# Every variable the entry points read. Copy to `.env` and fill it in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Never commit `.env`. It holds every credential: OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
# WEBHOOK_SECRET, IMAP_PASS and FREESCOUT_API_KEY.
#
# The minimum for a first run. DATABASE_URL and READER_DATABASE_URL below
# already name the compose stack the README's Quickstart starts. The migration
# reads both from this file, and the webhook, imap, consumer and relay read
# DATABASE_URL. The tests, the seeded history and the console read no `.env`,
# so the Quickstart exports both in the shell. Add
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY to run the consumer. A job that calls a model needs it
# too, and the README names what else each job needs.
#
# Most CLIs and daemons load `./.env` through `process.loadEnvFile`, so a
# variable already set in the shell always wins. Four commands read no `.env`
# file at all: `pnpm run escalations`, `pnpm run triage`,
# `pnpm run export-corpus` and `pnpm run seed-history`. The console reads none
# either. Export what they need in the shell.
#
# A value is commented out when the default is right for a dev stack.
# Uncomment to change it. A value that is NOT commented out has no default.
#
# Most such variables make the process refuse to start. WEBHOOK_SECRET is the
# exception and says so at its own entry: empty means the webhook verifies
# nothing. Read that entry before you deploy anything reachable.
#
# Which processes read what:
#
#   webhook   DATABASE_URL, WEBHOOK_HOST, WEBHOOK_PORT, WEBHOOK_SECRET,
#             PARTITION_COUNT, PRODUCTS_FILE
#   imap      DATABASE_URL, IMAP_HOST, IMAP_USER, IMAP_PASS, IMAP_PORT,
#             IMAP_SECURE, IMAP_INTERVAL_MS, PARTITION_COUNT, PRODUCTS_FILE
#   consumer  DATABASE_URL, CONSUMER_PARTITIONS, PARTITION_COUNT,
#             CONSUMER_INTERVAL_MS, TICKET_DEADLINE_MS, OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
#             OPENROUTER_BASE_URL, MODEL_VER, EMBED_MODEL_VER, PROMPT_VER,
#             EMBEDDINGS_USD_PER_MTOK, AURACLE_ZDR_ONLY, ACTION_CLASS,
#             ACTION_DELIVERY, ACTION_IS_CUSTOMER_VISIBLE, DISCLOSURE_TEXT,
#             EGRESS_ADAPTER and that adapter's variables
#   relay     DATABASE_URL, RELAY_ADAPTER, RELAY_INTERVAL_MS
#             and that adapter's variables
#   console   DATABASE_URL, READER_DATABASE_URL, CONSOLE_PORT, CONSOLE_HOST,
#             CONSOLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS, CONSOLE_IDENTITY_HEADER,
#             CONSOLE_DEV_REVIEWER, CONSOLE_HOLD_LAPSE_MS, CONSOLE_SEND_HOLD_MS,
#             PRODUCTS_FILE, APPROVE_ADAPTER and that adapter's variables
#   migrate   DATABASE_URL, READER_DATABASE_URL
#   latency   READER_DATABASE_URL, AURACLE_ALLOW_COMPOSE_DEFAULT
#
#   Every process above but migrate reads AURACLE_ALLOW_COMPOSE_DEFAULT too.
#   The latency report reads DATABASE_URL only when that variable is 1.
#
# The adapters' own variables:
#
#   freescout  FREESCOUT_BASE_URL, FREESCOUT_API_KEY, FREESCOUT_USER_ID,
#              FREESCOUT_INTERNAL_NOTE
#   imap-smtp  IMAP_HOST, IMAP_USER, IMAP_PASS, IMAP_PORT, IMAP_SECURE,
#              IMAP_DRAFTS_MAILBOX, SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_SECURE,
#              EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS
#
# Of the offline commands, load-docs reads DATABASE_URL,
# AURACLE_ALLOW_COMPOSE_DEFAULT, PRODUCTS_FILE, EMBED_MODEL_VER and
# EMBEDDINGS_USD_PER_MTOK. The reviewers command reads the first three. The
# jobs that call a model read OPENROUTER_* above. Each offline job's own
# variables are in the last section, under the command that reads them.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ledger ----

# The Postgres the ledger lives in. Every process but the latency report needs
# it. None of them guesses: a daemon that guessed would process real traffic
# against the wrong database and report health while doing it. Set it, or set
# AURACLE_ALLOW_COMPOSE_DEFAULT=1 for a local run against the compose stack.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://auracle:auracle@localhost:5433/auracle

# The same ledger, as the readers' role auracle_reader. The console's
# readers and `pnpm run latency` connect through it. Each read refuses any
# other role, so DATABASE_URL cannot stand in.
# `pnpm run db:migrate` sets the role's login to the password in this URL.
# Percent-encode @ / : ? # % and a space in the password. Use printable ASCII.
# The value below is the compose stack's local default.
READER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://auracle_reader:auracle_reader@localhost:5433/auracle

# The ledger's password in both compose files: the one Postgres sets when it
# first creates its data volume, and the one the dev stack's URLs carry. Read
# by compose, not by any process. Set it before the first `up` on a host that
# serves traffic; on an existing volume change the role's password as well.
# Use URL-safe characters only (A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~), because the URLs carry
# it unencoded and Postgres takes it raw. `openssl rand -hex 24` makes one.
# A process you start from a checkout reads DATABASE_URL above, so set the
# same password there too.
#AURACLE_DB_PASSWORD=

# The password docker-compose.dev.yml gives auracle_reader, in the URL it
# hands the migration and the console. Read by compose, not by any process.
# Set it on a host that serves traffic; empty keeps the local default.
# Use URL-safe characters only, as for AURACLE_DB_PASSWORD above.
#AURACLE_READER_PASSWORD=

# Accepts the compose default above when DATABASE_URL is unset. For a local
# run only. Do not set it on a host that serves traffic.
#AURACLE_ALLOW_COMPOSE_DEFAULT=1

# The image docker-compose.dev.yml runs. Set it to the one the release
# workflow pushed, ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>:<commit-sha>, so the host runs a
# named build and a rollback is a tag change. Read by compose, not by any
# process; the default builds from the checkout.
#AURACLE_IMAGE=auracle:local

# Which optional services docker-compose.dev.yml starts. `mail` adds the
# IMAP intake, which refuses to start without a mailbox — so a stack with no
# mailbox configured leaves it out rather than restarting it forever. Read
# by compose itself, not by any process.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=mail

# The host port docker-compose.yml publishes Postgres on. Bound to 127.0.0.1:
# the ledger is never published off-box. The default is 5433, so it does not
# collide with a local Postgres on 5432.
#AURACLE_DB_PORT=5433

# The host ports docker-compose.yml publishes the test mail server on, bound
# to 127.0.0.1. Read by compose, not by any process.
#AURACLE_SMTP_PORT=3025
#AURACLE_IMAP_PORT=3143

# The FreeScout administrator docker-compose.freescout.yml creates. Compose
# refuses to start that stack without both. Read by compose, not by any
# process. FREESCOUT_ADMIN_PASSWORD is a credential.
#FREESCOUT_ADMIN_EMAIL=
#FREESCOUT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- model ----

# The OpenRouter key. Every model call needs it, so the consumer and the
# offline jobs need it. The webhook, the IMAP intake, the relay and the
# console do not. Secret.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Point the provider somewhere else, for a proxy or a local gateway.
#OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# The chat model the consumer pins (DEC-26). The default lives in one place,
# src/config/env.ts, so the runbook can state it and a test can bind the two.
#MODEL_VER=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash

# The embedding model retrieval pins. Changing it invalidates every vector
# already in the ledger: the old and new spaces are not comparable, so
# re-index before you change it.
#EMBED_MODEL_VER=openai/text-embedding-3-small

# The prompt version recorded on every step (DEC-26). Change it when you
# change a prompt, so a run says which prompt produced it.
#PROMPT_VER=consumer-prompt-v1

# The DEC-17 data guarantee. Absence means off, because DEC-17 makes it
# configurable rather than forced. `1` sends every request zero-retention.
# Leaving it unset permits the provider to retain prompts, including the
# conversation history of DEC-37 — that is a policy choice, so make it.
#AURACLE_ZDR_ONLY=1


# ------------------------------------------------------------- partitions ----

# How many partitions the queue hashes into (DEC-36). Every process that
# enqueues or consumes must agree on this number. If the webhook uses 8 and
# the consumer uses 4, they disagree about which partition owns a
# conversation, and one partition ends up with two owners — which corrupts
# the ledger quietly rather than failing.
#PARTITION_COUNT=8

# Which partitions THIS consumer owns, comma separated. Required: the
# consumer refuses to start without it, because one consumer owns each
# partition (DEC-27) and a default would silently give two consumers the same
# one. A single consumer owns them all: 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7. Two consumers split
# the list and never overlap.
CONSUMER_PARTITIONS=0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7

# Milliseconds. The ceiling on a lost wake-up signal, not the normal queue
# wait: intake signals the consumer on enqueue and the loop wakes on it, so
# the usual wait is far below this.
#CONSUMER_INTERVAL_MS=5000

# Milliseconds a single ticket may take before the run is abandoned.
#TICKET_DEADLINE_MS=60000


# --------------------------------------------------- what a run may do ----
# The consumer's action profile (section 4.3). Read at startup, and every
# malformed value refuses to start rather than being corrected.

# The action class each run executes. `customer_reply` is the one class the
# relay sends as a draft. Every other class is an internal write.
#ACTION_CLASS=route_ticket

# `at_most_once` or `at_least_once`. Nothing else is accepted.
#ACTION_DELIVERY=at_most_once

# `true` marks the action customer visible, which sends every one of them
# through approval (DEC-12). It must be `true` for ACTION_CLASS=customer_reply
# and `false` for every other class. The consumer refuses any other pairing,
# because the relay cannot send the reply. A hidden action runs its writes
# without approval wherever the gates pass.
#ACTION_IS_CUSTOMER_VISIBLE=false

# The DEC-14 disclosure attached to a customer-visible reply. Required when
# ACTION_IS_CUSTOMER_VISIBLE=true, and the consumer refuses to start without
# it. The default below applies only when the action is not customer visible.
# A blank value counts as unset: a visible profile refuses it, and a hidden one
# takes the default.
#DISCLOSURE_TEXT=This reply was drafted by an automated assistant and reviewed by a person.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- intake ----

# The port the webhook binds.
#WEBHOOK_PORT=8080

# The interface the webhook binds. Loopback by default, because the webhook
# accepts every request while WEBHOOK_SECRET is empty. `docker-compose.dev.yml`
# sets 0.0.0.0 inside the container and publishes the port on loopback. A
# blank value refuses the start, because Node binds every interface for it.
# So does a value with spaces around it.
#WEBHOOK_HOST=127.0.0.1

# The channel map (DEC-47): which channel serves which product. The webhook
# and the IMAP intake read it at startup and refuse to start when it cannot
# be read, so a message never enqueues with a product nothing can resolve.
# A ticket whose channel the map does not name records no product, and a run
# with no product retrieves only documents that declare none.
#PRODUCTS_FILE=products.json

# The shared secret the webhook verifies on each request.
#
# ABSENT MEANS OPEN, and this file ships it empty. The driver starts, logs
# `webhook_unauthenticated`, and accepts every request that reaches the
# port — each one enqueuing a ticket that spends the model budget. That is
# useful on a laptop and wrong anywhere else, which is why
# `docker-compose.dev.yml` publishes the webhook on loopback only. Fill this
# in before you put anything in front of it. Secret.
WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The mailbox the IMAP intake reads. All three are required and the process
# refuses to start without them, so leave this block empty unless you run the
# mail path. IMAP_PASS is a credential; it is sent verbatim, and a trailing
# space inside quotes is kept deliberately rather than trimmed.
#IMAP_HOST=
#IMAP_PORT=993
#IMAP_USER=
#IMAP_PASS=
# Write `true` or `false`, and nothing else. The two readers disagree about
# every other spelling: the imap daemon treats anything but the literal
# `false` as TLS, and the imap-smtp adapter accepts only true/1/false/0 and
# defaults the other way. So `IMAP_SECURE=0` means TLS to one and no TLS to
# the other.
#IMAP_SECURE=true
# Milliseconds between mailbox polls.
#IMAP_INTERVAL_MS=60000


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- egress ----

# Which adapter the consumer parks a draft through, and which the relay sends
# through. `helpdesk`, `imap-smtp` or `freescout`; an unknown name refuses.
#
# THE DEFAULT DELIVERS NOTHING. `helpdesk` is the reference adapter: it holds
# fixtures, opens no connection, and answers no customer. A stack left on the
# default parks and sends into memory while every health signal stays green.
#
# They are two variables because they are two processes, and they must name
# the same adapter. A consumer that parks through imap-smtp while the relay
# runs helpdesk leaves the relay looking in the wrong system. APPROVE_ADAPTER
# must name it too: a console reading back through another adapter cannot read
# the drafts it finds, and refuses every approval.
#
# A hidden ACTION_CLASS must be one that EGRESS_ADAPTER performs. `helpdesk`
# performs close_ticket, route_ticket and compensate; imap-smtp and freescout
# perform none. The consumer refuses to start otherwise. On the helpdesk it
# refuses compensate, because the consumer's payload carries no reason.
#EGRESS_ADAPTER=helpdesk
#RELAY_ADAPTER=helpdesk
# The approval reads the parked draft back through this adapter. The CLI does
# so under --read-back. The console does so whenever it names imap-smtp or
# freescout; unset or helpdesk reads nothing back, and records no approval-edit
# distance.
#APPROVE_ADAPTER=helpdesk

# Milliseconds between outbox drains.
#RELAY_INTERVAL_MS=5000

# The imap-smtp adapter, when EGRESS_ADAPTER or RELAY_ADAPTER names it. It
# reads the four IMAP_* values above as well, and unlike the imap daemon it
# requires IMAP_PORT and defaults IMAP_SECURE to false.
#SMTP_HOST=
#SMTP_PORT=
#SMTP_SECURE=true
# The support address an approved reply is sent from.
#EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=
# The mailbox a gated draft is parked in (DEC-11).
#IMAP_DRAFTS_MAILBOX=Drafts

# The FreeScout adapter, when EGRESS_ADAPTER or RELAY_ADAPTER names it. All
# three are required in that case. FREESCOUT_API_KEY is a credential.
#FREESCOUT_BASE_URL=
#FREESCOUT_API_KEY=
#FREESCOUT_USER_ID=

# DEC-44: set this only after your own instance proves it accepts an internal
# note. With it, the park is a note no customer can be sent, so the ledger
# holds the approved reply and the console lets a reviewer edit it. Without
# it, the park stays a draft thread and the draft is what sends. The REST API
# lives in FreeScout's paid "API & Webhooks" module: a stock instance answers
# 404 to every /api/ route, so nothing but a working instance can prove it.
#
# Every process that reads this must agree: the consumer that parks, the relay
# that sends, and the console that approves. A relay without it cannot send a
# reply the console recorded, so it escalates that record rather than post the
# copy a person edited.
#FREESCOUT_INTERNAL_NOTE=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- console ----

# The port the console binds. A value outside 1-65535 is refused rather than
# silently binding an ephemeral port nobody can find.
#CONSOLE_PORT=8787

# The interface to bind. The default reaches nothing outside the machine. In
# a container that is not a safety boundary — it publishes a port that
# answers nothing — so the compose file sets 0.0.0.0 and limits reach with
# the port mapping instead. A blank value or one with spaces around it
# refuses the start, because Node binds every interface for an empty host.
#CONSOLE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# The Host headers the console answers to, comma separated, each a host and a
# port rather than a URL. Setting it REPLACES the names derived from
# CONSOLE_HOST and CONSOLE_PORT rather than adding to them.
#
# Required behind a proxy. Without it the console derives loopback names
# only, so a browser reaching it by the host's name sends a Host the console
# refuses. The console holds no credential of its own, so that proxy is what
# authenticates the reviewer (DEC-45).
#CONSOLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# The header a trusted proxy sets to name the reviewer (DEC-42). Naming it is
# you stating that a proxy you trust sets it and strips any copy a client
# sent. Unset means no proxy names anyone, so the console serves nothing until
# a proxy sets this header, or CONSOLE_DEV_REVIEWER names an identity.
#
# The console refuses to start if this names a header a client sets for itself
# (cookie, host, user-agent and the like), or one that carries a credential
# rather than a name (authorization and the assertions an authenticating proxy
# forwards). Have the proxy name the reviewer in a header of its own, such as
# X-Auth-Request-Email. It also refuses a value set beside CONSOLE_DEV_REVIEWER.
#CONSOLE_IDENTITY_HEADER=

# How long a reservation lasts after a reviewer takes a case (DEC-46), in
# milliseconds. The default is twenty minutes. A reservation lapses when this
# passes with no further action from the reviewer, and the case reads as free.
#CONSOLE_HOLD_LAPSE_MS=1200000

# How long a batch's sends wait before the relay may post them (DEC-51), in
# milliseconds. The default is twelve seconds, which is what the console's
# undo button counts down. A reviewer who confirms a group by mistake undoes
# it inside this window, and nothing has reached a customer. Set 0 to send at
# once and offer no undo. Five minutes is the longest the console starts with:
# the relay skips a held record, so a longer one waits in silence. The
# console, the relay and the consumer all read this ledger, so a value here is
# the whole stack's: the hold is on the record the relay reads, not on the
# page.
#CONSOLE_SEND_HOLD_MS=12000

# An identity to serve as, for a console run from source on loopback
# (DEC-45). Use it with `pnpm run dev:console`, and never in .env: the compose
# stack mounts this file into a console that runs from the image and binds
# 0.0.0.0, so it refuses to start and restarts in a loop. The roster must hold
# the identity as well, and the console refuses to start until it does:
#   pnpm run reviewers -- --add <identity>
#CONSOLE_DEV_REVIEWER=


# ----------------------------------------------------- offline jobs only ----
# No long-running process reads anything below this line, except the
# consumer, which reads EMBEDDINGS_USD_PER_MTOK.

# `pnpm run fit-groundedness` and `pnpm run report-groundedness`.
# GROUNDEDNESS_MODEL_VER has no default and the job refuses without it.
#GROUNDEDNESS_MODEL_VER=
#GROUNDEDNESS_PROMPT_VER=ground-p1
#GROUNDEDNESS_LIMIT=
#GROUNDEDNESS_RESUME=

# `pnpm run calibrate`, `pnpm run gold-report`, `pnpm run fit-groundedness`
# and `pnpm run report-groundedness`: an adopter's own labeled history, in
# place of the committed corpus.
# Set both or neither: naming one half without the other is refused, because
# DEC-31 needs two halves to prove they are disjoint. Keep them under the
# gitignored `corpus/`, because real history never enters this repository
# (DEC-18).
#GOLD_FIT_PATH=
#GOLD_REPORT_PATH=

# `pnpm run calibrate`.
#CALIBRATION_MODEL_VER=
#CALIBRATION_TARGET_PRECISION=

# `pnpm run export-corpus` and `pnpm run induce`.
#CORPUS_PATH=
#CORPUS_FETCH_CMD=
#CORPUS_AGENT_ADDRESSES=
#CHAT_MODEL_VER=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# `pnpm run load-docs`. Micro-dollars are the ledger's unit; this one is US
# dollars per million tokens, which is how a provider quotes it. Every process
# that calls the model reads it, for a gateway whose embedding answer carries
# no cost; OpenRouter reports the cost itself. Blank means unset.
#EMBEDDINGS_USD_PER_MTOK=

# The GreenMail server the mail suites drive. The README's Quickstart exports
# MAIL_HOST=127.0.0.1 for the compose server, and CI sets all three. Unset, a
# test run starts a throwaway container.
#MAIL_HOST=
#MAIL_SMTP_PORT=
#MAIL_IMAP_PORT=

# Keep a warm database or mail server between local test runs.
#AURACLE_TEST_DB_REUSE=1
#AURACLE_TEST_MAIL_REUSE=1

# A test run with DATABASE_URL set restates auracle_reader with a known
# password. It refuses a server on another host unless this is 1.
# Set it only when that server is disposable.
#AURACLE_TEST_ALLOW_REMOTE_DB=1

# Accept every end-to-end artifact that differs from its committed golden.
# Set it for one run, review `git diff -- src/e2e/artifacts`, then commit.
#AURACLE_E2E_ACCEPT=1
